-- Regression test for lexical section scoping — FIX D (the other half of FIX B).
--
-- A section's OWN reference to a name must resolve to the nearest
-- ancestor-or-self binding even when a DESCENDANT section further down the
-- file rebinds the same name. Spec §3.1: same section first, then ancestors,
-- then top level; a child's rebinding is not on its parent's ancestry.
--
-- Previously the descendant's binding, not yet inferred when the ancestor's
-- reference was checked, still carried an unresolved inference-variable type
-- (a fully annotated ASSUME included: a signature's type is only unified with
-- its declaration during inference). It therefore fell into its own type
-- group, where the flat-scope fallback kept it, and the ancestor's own
-- reference reported
--
--     There are multiple definitions for the identifier x
--
-- naming the ancestor's binding and the descendant's. The same rebinding
-- placed ABOVE the reference resolved fine, so the answer depended on textual
-- order. FIX B (spec §12) already let a wildcard ANCESTOR shadow farther
-- ancestors; this pins the missing complement: a wildcard that is OFF the
-- ancestry never out-competes a value ancestor.

-- 1. Top level vs. a section below it (spec §5.2).
x MEANS 10
`top f` MEANS x               -- 10: top-level x, although § a rebinds x

§ a
x MEANS 1
`a f` MEANS x                 -- 1: a's own x, although §§ b and §§§ c rebind x
ASSUME y IS A NUMBER
#CHECK y                      -- NUMBER: a's y, although §§ b rebinds y

§§ b
x MEANS 2
ASSUME y IS A NUMBER
`b f` MEANS x                 -- 2: b's own x, although §§§ c rebinds x
`b via a` MEANS a.x           -- 1: qualified access unchanged (spec §3.2)

§§§ c
x MEANS 3
`c f` MEANS x                 -- 3

#EVAL `top f`     -- 10
#EVAL `a f`       -- 1
#EVAL `b f`       -- 2
#EVAL `b via a`   -- 1
#EVAL `c f`       -- 3
